XXI. The hierarchy I'd actually implement

I wouldn't implement all ~190 concepts simultaneously.

I'd build the mathematical kernel roughly like this:

Logic
↓
Sets + Functions
↓
Algebra
↓
Linear Algebra
↓
Calculus
↓
Differential Equations
↓
Dynamical Systems
↓
Topology
↓
Algebraic Topology
↓
Probability + Statistics
↓
Information Theory
↓
Optimization
↓
Program Synthesis
↓
Control
	​

	​


with geometry, graph theory, signal processing, category theory, and domain-specific mathematics branching horizontally.

The resulting idea is considerably larger than "an AI that solves equations."

It is closer to:

a computational mathematical universe in which programs are mathematical objects.
	​


The agent observes a state space, constructs mathematical representations of it, searches a space of possible transformations, verifies those transformations against mathematical invariants, optimizes them against objectives, compiles the surviving structure into executable procedures, and feeds the consequences back into the next generation.

That is the coherent mathematical foundation I would use for the system you've been describing.
